science to try to try to advance the  field of brain cancer I drove some of  the most sophisticated machine around  that time this is a particular cotton  picker and as you can see I was a full  life so full of energy and this is  actually a machine that always get me  excited to talk about it because right  here in the inside of this machine as  you can see you can actually sit not  much different than the way I see today  in my operating room chair just like an  astronaut chair and you're controlling  this big machine that is picking cotton  with both of your hands and both of your  fits very very early on I was having the  practice that was gonna allow me one day  to be able to do brain surgery  completely connected to my patients the  only difference nowadays is at the  microscope I also control it with my  mouth and I put a mouthpiece that I  control and just like back then I used  to drive this machine now I also control  the microscope with my mouth and I can  zoom  zoom out move the microscope from side  to side and be able to navigate the  brain back then I was navigating the  cotton fields now I've been privileged  to be given the opportunity but my  patients and their families to be able  to navigate the brain I was an  entrepreneur I took risks early on I  began my own trucking company this is a  truck that I bought and I had a dream  that one day I was gonna have a whole  fleet of trucks that was gonna allow me  to carry cotton from place to place some  of the products in the San Joaquin  Valley so early on I was getting all  ready there early teachings of taking  risk unfortunately things don't always  work the way you want it and as you can  see this truck I actually ended up  getting completely damaged not only  because I myself could actually fix the  engine that eventually completely blew  off because I didn't know much about  mechanics but I began to learn through  reading a book but the lessons that I  learned back then is never to give up  and always to try to try to push the  field forward and I push the field  forward with this little truck back then  by trying to fix it by trying to fix the  engine by trying to fix the ability of  this truck to work in the fields  eventually I actually ended up leaving  the field of the San Joaquin Valley I  was told once but one of my co-workers  thought I was gonna spend the rest of my  life working in the fields just like  many generations of Mexican Americans  who had immigrated to the San Joaquin  Valley I was going on 20 years or so  full of life so full of hope so full of  energy and to be told this is not  something that I was willing to accept  by the time I was in Berkeley I remember  I was given a cart by one of my  classmates with a court from Cesar  Chavez that said if you're not  frightened that you might fail you'll  never do the job if you are frightened  you will work like crazy in like crazy I  have worked my whole life not only as a  young child working in the  station of my parents but now as an  adult now as currently as a brain tumor  surgeon and as a scientist trying to  push the field in April 14 1989 just a  couple of years before I went to UC  Berkeley I had a major event in my life  in which I almost lost my life and I  remember waking up and seeing my father  after everything was under control and I  was out of danger he told me that I was  given a precious gift that life was  short just to be good to others this is  an early picture of me in the middle  with my brother Gabriel and one of my  co-workers in which I was working in  this railroad company in which I almost  left my life however I never gave up and  I never never was afraid of continuing  continuing to work really hard towards a  dream a dream that I was gonna make it  not only better for myself and my family  but potentially for future generations I  always tell my students that there is a  fine line between a being a mentor and  being a tormentor and I can personally  tell you that I proudly cross that line  my first mentor was Joe Martinez this is  a picture in 1993 in one of his  laboratory meters in his house I was  given the opportunity by dr. Martinez to  be part of science and since then my  life began he was an influential figure  in my life  this is 1994 by the time I graduated  from UC Berkeley with honors I ended up  following what my grandfather used to  tell me as a young child do not go where  the path may lead to go instead where  there is no path and leave a trail and  thanks to dr. Martinez I was inspired to  then pursue my medical education at  Harvard Medical School where I met two  other great mentors Dave Potter and Edie  Kravitz dr. Potter and dr. Kravitz were  not only role models but  people who believed that I could  actually make a difference in science  but I never forgot where I came from  this is a picture that I did with two of  my classmates miguel Canales and Reuben  Fragoso in which we have symbolisms of  the Aztec culture by the Sun in which  we're all moving upwards in our lives  but turning back in trying to help  others that are behind this is the  theory of life scatter Rita which is the  ladder the ladder to move upwards in our  society I strongly believe that  education is the best provision for old  age I was told once by the time I  graduated from Harvard Here I am in  Harvard Medical School given the  commencement speech chosen by my  classmates and I was reminded what Vince  Lombardi once said the quality of a  person's life is in direct proportion to  their commitment to excellence  regardless of the chosen field of  endeavor here I was now as a resident  being doctor by my own daughter outside  of the operating room  and in the operating room using some of  the most sophisticated equipment to be  able to perform brain surgery I pursue  research and you will learn more in my  next segment of brain tumors and stem  cells but I was able to work with  astrocytes stars that lie within the  brain that we believe our stem cells how  little that I'm that I know that that  little boy that work in this little gas  station at the age of five was one day  going to be surrounded by incredible  stars this is a picture a few day a few  years ago of the graduation of some of  my residents and to my right is that for  Alessandro O'Leary then if you look  further is dr. Henry Bram and then right  next to that for Henry Bram is one of  the greatest icons of the  african-american medicine which is dr.  ben Carson I've been surrounded by star  this is a picture of astrocytes that I  use in my research I get to do and use  the  sophisticated equipment in the upper  in-room because I believe that my role  as a physician and surgeon and scientist  is not only to give hope inside of the  operating room but also outside of the  operating room through our work the  brain I was told once is not a vessel to  be filled but a fire to be ignited we  have a high dropout rate by the year  2050 we're going to be one of the  largest groups Hispanic population of  this country one out of four people by  2050 would be a Hispanic descent if we  don't educate them we will not be able  to continue to sustain this beautiful  country so I believe that that through  the termination dedication discipline  resilience excitement admiration and  mentorship we can do great things  together  success Winston Churchill once said is  going from failure to failure without  the loss of enthusiasm I tell my own  children that sometimes we fall so we  can have an opportunity to get up and  try again I tell my own children the  lessons that I learned from my own  grandfather that a fool with a good tool  is still a fool that is not the tool  that matters but what you do with it  and your education is a tool and the  better you use it the better we will be  able to keep this country as beautiful  as it is today I am thankful for the  people that have surrounded me all these  young people that keep me energized in  vibrant and up-to-date not only in  science but also in education and  medicine and by continuing to give hope  to my patients I thank you for your  attention dr. quinones here from Johns  Hopkins  you
 

